          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The rule, again. (Take the they past, swap -ron for -ra.)"
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Now try it on the verbs that gave you the most trouble."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: the hard ones cost nothing",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "they past",
                "supposing"
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 4,
              "utterances": [
                "they past: tuvieron. supposing: tuviera.",
                "they past: hicieron. supposing: hiciera.",
                "they past: estuvieron. supposing: estuviera.",
                "they past: fueron. supposing: fuera."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Nothing new was learned here. The same swap, applied to stems you already had. The four words you memorised for the preterite have now paid for themselves twice."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And fuera keeps the double duty fui and fueron had: it is the supposing form of ser and of ir. Si fuera can begin if I were or if I went, and only the rest of the sentence decides."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "culture-pragmatics",
          "title": "Why it's said this way",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "This is what a well-shaped irregularity looks like."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Spanish could have given the supposing form its own set of broken verbs. Instead it built the form out of one that was already broken, so the damage is paid for once and reused. Learn tuvieron and you have tuviera for nothing."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Which is why the plural had to come first. It was never only about saying they had."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
